From 8aaa9d03906de936cbde13aae6b50367b3e20b24 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 03:04:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(catalog): enforce OpenRouter exclusion across every
 catalog path
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The merged ROX public-models change filtered the catalog with a predicate that
only recognized the legacy The Old LLM ids (`openrouter_gpt_4_o`), while the
builder emits canonical records (`openrouter/<vendor>/<model>`, owned_by
"openrouter"). The filter was a no-op for live and synced OpenRouter entries, so
/v1/models kept publishing them.

Three paths republished OpenRouter or raw ids:

- the global filter missed every canonical entry;
- the DB-backed key branch iterated the pre-policy `models` collection, so a key
  whose permissions named an OpenRouter model added it back after it had been
  removed;
- the quota-exclusive short circuit returns before the ROX public projection, so
  a quota key was served `qtSd/*` ids even with ROX_PUBLIC_CATALOG_ONLY on.

The predicate now matches ownership exactly and model paths per segment, per-key
filtering runs on the policy-filtered list, and the exclusion is re-applied in
finalizeCatalogResponse — the single exit both catalog paths share — so a branch
added later cannot republish OpenRouter by omission.

openrouter-vision-sync-4264 and specialty-model-hidden-openrouter-9293 asserted
that OpenRouter models are published, which is what this fork forbids. #4264's
catalog coverage moves to another provider on the same synced path; #9293 keeps
its hidden-flag assertions and now pins the exclusion instead.
